  <info><![CDATA[## WHAT IS IT?

MUTUS (Mutualism in Texts and Understanding of Students) is a minimal agent-based model of a **mutualistic network between two guilds**. One computational core supports two worlds:
 Meadow with pollinators (bumblebees), flowering plants,  proboscis length vs. corolla dept 
or Wiki with students of a course, articles of a course wiki or glossary,  competence vs. conceptual difficulty (zone of proximal development



The model asks which regularities of plant-pollinator networks transfer to collaborative text production, and which are changed by processes that are slow or absent in ecology: learning within the ZPD, scaffolding by text quality, and authoring of new texts by the learners themselves.

MUTUS is built as a **ladder of six levels**. Each level adds one mechanism, and each mechanism has its own switch, so the contribution of every mechanism can be isolated in BehaviorSpace.

## HOW IT WORKS

Each tick, every active forager forages with probability equal to its activity:

1. **Perceives** the resources within `perception-radius` (its landscape or its topical horizon).
2. **Chooses** a partner by roulette. The weight is the forager's effort on the resource (M3) times the resource's vitality (M2); in M0вЂ“M1 the choice is neutral.
3. **Matches traits** (M1). Let d = effective complexity в€’ skill. If d > zpd-above the resource is too hard and the visit fails. If d < в€’zpd-below it is too easy: the forager gains little and learns nothing. Otherwise the visit falls inside the ZPD band.
4. **Consumes** reward (Holling type II; in the wiki, re-reading the same text yields less) and pays `visit-cost` in energy. Foragers with too little energy become dormant (in the wiki: drop out) and recover slowly.
5. **Serves** the resource, with probability equal to its energy, using the pollen (concepts) carried from the previous successful visit (M2). Service raises the quality and vitality of the resource; transfer between clusters is weighted by the cross-cluster efficiency of the world.
6. **Adapts** its effort towards rewarding partners (M3, replicator dynamics after Valdovinos et al. 2013).
7. **Learns** (M4): inside the band, skill grows by `skill-growth` Г— novelty Г— position in the band Г— (1 в€’ skill). Learning is fastest at the upper edge of the ZPD.

Then resources regenerate reward, fade without visits (vitality, and quality in the wiki) and update their effective complexity (scaffolding in the wiki). Faded resources can be **renewed** (M5a). In the meadow the newcomer is a seedling of a pollinated plant of the same cluster and inherits its complexity. In the wiki it is a new article written by a nearby active student at the upper edge of that student's ZPD.



## HOW TO USE IT

1. Choose `world-type` (meadow or wiki).
2. Choose the level with the switches:
 
Any combination of switches is valid. M4 needs M1 (without the ZPD band there is nothing to learn from), and M5a needs M2 (without feedback nothing fades).

3. Press **setup**, then **go**. A run lasts 150 ticks (one season or one semester) and stops by itself.

**Sliders:** `num-foragers`, `num-resources`, `cluster-count`, `cluster-spread`, `perception-radius`, `activity-mean`, `activity-concentration`, `visit-cost`, `mutualism-strength`, `regeneration-rate`, `adaptation-rate`, `skill-growth`, `renewal-rate`, `offspring-sd`.

**View:** resources are flowers (meadow) or squares (wiki), coloured by cluster, sized by vitality, and grey when faded. Foragers are bugs (meadow) or persons (wiki), shaded by skill, and grey when dormant.

**Monitors and plots** show active foragers, alive resources, failure and too-easy shares, mean skill and quality, distinct links, the skillвЂ“complexity gap and the number of renewals.

**Experiments.** Six BehaviorSpace experiments (E0вЂ“E5) are included. All output is collected with BehaviorSpace reporters, one table per experiment, and analysed with the R script `MUTUS_analysis.R` (see the README of the release).

## THINGS TO NOTICE

- With trait matching on, a third of the visits fail at first. Watch how adaptive foraging and learning reduce failures.
- With feedback on, some resources prosper and others fade: mutual benefit creates winners and losers.
- With learning on, the share of **too-easy** visits grows over the semester: learners outgrow their resources.
- With renewal on, compare the skillвЂ“complexity gap in the two worlds. In the wiki new articles follow the learners; in the meadow new flowers stay accessible.
- Around tick 80 the share of alive resources drops suddenly. All resources start with the same vitality, so the ones nobody visits fade together.

## THINGS TO TRY

- Set `perception-radius` to 3 and then to 12. How do dropout and cross-cluster visits change in each world?
- Raise `visit-cost`. Which world loses its foragers first?
- Switch learning on and off with renewal on. When does the wiki keep its students at the frontier of the material?
- Run M0 and M1 side by side: how much of the network structure does trait matching create?

## EXTENDING THE MODEL

- **M5b:** retire or rewrite resources that the learners have outgrown, not only those that have faded.
- Random initial vitality, to remove the synchronous fading at tick в‰€ 80.
- Recruitment and departure of foragers (students joining and leaving a course).
- Calibration of the wiki world against edit histories of real course wikis.

## NETLOGO FEATURES

Two breeds (`foragers`, `resources`). The model avoids links: the bipartite visit network is accumulated in lists and reported as strings (`web-edgelist`, `visit-sequence`, `renewal-list`) for analysis in R. In BehaviorSpace the random seed is the run number, so every table is reproducible.

## RELATED MODELS

- STiMUS v2.1 and STiMUS-HAI v3.1 (CoMSES): stigmergicвЂ“mutualistic models of teamwork on shared digital artefacts; MUTUS descends from STiMUS.
- Valdovinos et al. (2013, 2016): adaptive foraging in pollination networks.

## CREDITS AND REFERENCES

Author: Yevgeny Patarakin (Institute of Education, HSE University; Institute of Digital Education, Moscow City University).

Full description: `MUTUS_ODD_v1.0.md` (ODD protocol, Grimm et al. 2020).

- Bascompte, J., Jordano, P., MeliГЎn, C. J., & Olesen, J. M. (2003). The nested assembly of plantвЂ“animal mutualistic networks. PNAS, 100(16), 9383вЂ“9387. https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1633576100
- Grimm, V., et al. (2020). The ODD protocol for describing agent-based and other simulation models: a second update. JASSS, 23(2), 7. https://www.jasss.org/23/2/7.html
- Olesen, J. M., Bascompte, J., Dupont, Y. L., & Jordano, P. (2007). The modularity of pollination networks. PNAS, 104(50), 19891вЂ“19896. https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0706375104
- St-Onge, J., et al. (2022). Socio-semantic networks as mutualistic networks. Scientific Reports, 12, 1889. https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8813919/
- Valdovinos, F. S., et al. (2013). Adaptive foraging allows the maintenance of biodiversity of pollination networks. Oikos, 122, 907вЂ“917.
- Vygotsky, L. S. (1978). Mind in Society. Harvard University Press.
- Wilensky, U. (1999). NetLogo. Center for Connected Learning and Computer-Based Modeling, Northwestern University. https://ccl.northwestern.edu/netlogo/
]]></info>
